1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,450
Welcome to Module Twenty-Five.

2
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:06,537
"It takes all the running you can do, to keep in the same place."

3
00:00:06,537 --> 00:00:11,050
You know why evolutionary biologists borrowed that line from the Red Queen.

4
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:16,000
Hosts and parasites, predators and prey, keep each other honest.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,587
Take the predators away, and the prey slowly lose their vigilance.

6
00:00:20,587 --> 00:00:23,475
A defence that is never tested decays.

7
00:00:23,475 --> 00:00:25,712
AI systems need opponents too.

8
00:00:25,712 --> 00:00:29,175
And today's rule is the most important one in this unit.

9
00:00:29,175 --> 00:00:32,300
Never let the generator grade its own homework.

10
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:33,550
Here is why.

11
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:40,637
Research cited in one of today's sources shows that language models can recognise their own writing, and that they tend to prefer it.

12
00:00:40,637 --> 00:00:45,150
So when a model checks its own work, it is a lenient referee by design.

13
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:48,187
One builder in the pack described exactly this.

14
00:00:48,187 --> 00:00:52,512
Every worker in their system checked its own output, and everything looked clean.

15
00:00:52,512 --> 00:00:54,162
Then they split the job.

16
00:00:54,162 --> 00:00:59,075
A separate checker, with one task: try to kill each finding before it moves on.

17
00:00:59,075 --> 00:01:01,350
Three details made that checker work.

18
00:01:01,350 --> 00:01:03,362
First, a fresh context.

19
00:01:03,362 --> 00:01:10,687
When the checker was handed the worker's whole conversation, it agreed with everything, because it had already been talked into the answer.

20
00:01:10,687 --> 00:01:13,700
Second, several lenses and a vote.

21
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:20,575
One checker looks at correctness, one at the sources, one at overclaiming, and the majority decides.

22
00:01:20,575 --> 00:01:23,612
Third, an anchor outside the models.

23
00:01:23,612 --> 00:01:29,725
If every checker compares a number against the same summary, their agreement is just an echo.

24
00:01:29,725 --> 00:01:35,125
The anchor is the original source, the raw data, or a test that actually runs.

25
00:01:35,125 --> 00:01:37,087
And then, test the tester.

26
00:01:37,087 --> 00:01:40,700
Break something on purpose and make sure your checker notices.

27
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:47,462
If a deliberately broken answer passes, your checker is blind, and every green tick after that means nothing.

28
00:01:47,462 --> 00:01:49,650
In the lab, you'll do exactly that.

29
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:55,725
Your coding agent will write a short report of twelve claims, each citing a passage from this pack.

30
00:01:55,725 --> 00:02:05,262
A script will then plant six errors: a changed number, a reversed claim, a wrong citation, and others, including one typical of your own field.

31
00:02:05,262 --> 00:02:07,775
Then three checkers try to find them.

32
00:02:07,775 --> 00:02:09,675
The writer checking itself.

33
00:02:09,675 --> 00:02:13,987
An independent verifier that sees only the claim and its source.

34
00:02:13,987 --> 00:02:18,550
And an adversary that tries three different ways to disprove each claim.

35
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:25,550
You'll count what each one catches, and what each one wrongly flags, and you'll put a confidence interval on every number.

36
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:29,300
With only six planted errors, those intervals will be wide.

37
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:31,087
That's part of the lesson.

38
00:02:31,087 --> 00:02:37,475
Robotics labs now publish success rates from thousands of trials, with intervals, for exactly this reason.

39
00:02:37,475 --> 00:02:44,787
Start with Part A. Think of one mistake you often see in papers in your field, and write it down as your sixth error type.

